The model syntax read by \code{\link[=fit_sem]{fit_sem()}} and by \code{fit_mediation(engine = "native")}. It is a lavaan-style core plus two extensions, each marked
"medfit extension" below. One statement per line (or separated by \verb{;}).
}
\section{Core statements}{
\tabular{ll}{
   Statement \tab Meaning \cr
   \code{y ~ x1 + x2} \tab regression of \code{y} on \code{x1} and \code{x2} \cr
   \code{f =~ y1 + y2 + y3} \tab latent variable \code{f} measured by the indicators \cr
   \code{y1 ~~ y2} \tab covariance; \code{y1 ~~ y1} a variance \cr
   \code{y ~ a*x} \tab label the path \code{a}; equal labels are one parameter \cr
   \code{y ~ 0.5*x} \tab fix the path at 0.5 \cr
   \code{y ~ start(0.3)*x}, \verb{lower(0)*}, \verb{upper(1)*} \tab starting value, lower and upper bound \cr
   \code{a == b}, \code{a + b == 1} \tab equality constraint on labels \cr
   \code{a < b}, \code{a > 0} \tab inequality constraint; \code{label < number} is a bound \cr
   \code{ab := a*b} \tab defined parameter, with a delta-method standard error \cr
}


Constraints must be linear in the labels. Labels used in a constraint or a
definition must be defined in the model. A constraint with no free parameter,
or a nonlinear one, is an error. Exogenous variables are given free
variances and covariances, as lavaan does with \code{fixed.x = FALSE}. Mean
structures (\code{y ~ 1}) are not supported, except that \code{y ~ 0*1} is accepted.
}

\section{medfit extensions}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Comma shorthand} (medfit extension). A comma-separated left side
expands to one statement per name: \verb{y1, y2 ~ x} means \code{y1 ~ x} and
\code{y2 ~ x}. Commas on the right side are not supported; use \code{+}.
\strong{lavaan reads \verb{y1, y2 ~ x} differently: it silently keeps only \code{y2}.}
A model copied between the two packages can therefore change meaning
without an error.
\item \strong{\code{CONSTRAINT(...)}} (medfit extension). A constraint line may be
written \code{CONSTRAINT(a == b)}, equivalent to the bare \code{a == b}. The keyword
is case-sensitive and starts the statement, one pair of parentheses
encloses the whole constraint, it holds exactly one comparison, and no
text follows the closing parenthesis.
}
}

\section{Expressions}{

Both sides of a constraint and the right side of a definition are
arithmetic expressions in the labels, with \verb{+ - * / ^}, parentheses, finite
numbers, and the functions \code{exp}, \code{log}, \code{log10}, \code{sqrt}, \code{abs}, \code{min}, \code{max},
\code{sin}, \code{cos}, \code{tan}, \code{pnorm} and \code{qnorm}. Anything else is
rejected with the construct named.
}

